      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Back-related leg pain (BRLP) is often disabling and costly, and there is a paucity of research to guide its management."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine whether spinal manipulative therapy (SMT) plus home exercise and advice (HEA) compared with HEA alone reduces leg pain in the short and long term in adults with BRLP."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Controlled pragmatic trial with allocation by minimization conducted from 2007 to 2011. (ClinicalTrials.gov: NCT00494065)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "2 research centers (Minnesota and Iowa)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Persons aged 21 years or older with BRLP for least 4 weeks."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "12 weeks of SMT plus HEA or HEA alone."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
          "content": "The primary outcome was patient-rated BRLP at 12 and 52 weeks. Secondary outcomes were self-reported low back pain, disability, global improvement, satisfaction, medication use, and general health status at 12 and 52 weeks. Blinded objective tests were done at 12 weeks."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Of the 192 enrolled patients, 191 (99%) provided follow-up data at 12 weeks and 179 (93%) at 52 weeks. For leg pain, SMT plus HEA had a clinically important advantage over HEA (difference, 10 percentage points [95% CI, 2 to 19]; P=0.008) at 12 weeks but not at 52 weeks (difference, 7 percentage points [CI, -2 to 15]; P=0.146). Nearly all secondary outcomes improved more with SMT plus HEA at 12 weeks, but only global improvement, satisfaction, and medication use had sustained improvements at 52 weeks. No serious treatment-related adverse events or deaths occurred."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "LIMITATION",
          "content": "Patients and providers could not be blinded."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "For patients with BRLP, SMT plus HEA was more effective than HEA alone after 12 weeks, but the benefit was sustained only for some secondary outcomes at 52 weeks."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "PRIMARY FUNDING SOURCE",
          "content": "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services."
        }
      ]},
